<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>Quiz 4 - MTH 305 - Regan</title>

    <p>
        This quiz covers retakes of Quiz #1, 2, and 3.
        Read all <term>DIRECTIONS</term> carefully.
        Good luck!
    </p>

    <p>
        <term> Retake of Quiz 3:</term>
    </p>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Suppose you have a model that depends on a parameter <m>c</m>.
                The following data gives the relative minima for each choice of
                parameter <m>c</m>.
                The <m>c</m> parameter is changing by approximately <m>2\%</m>
                each time.
            </p>

            <image source="images/quiz3_1.png" width="80%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Data of relative minima for differing c parameter values for
                    the Zika mosquito model.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the percent change in the relative minima.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Based on your results, do you believe the model to be
                    sensitive to the parameter <m>c</m>?
                    Why or why not?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>k</m> is a constant that captures the intrinsic
                strength of a square wooden beam relative to its width (
                <m>w</m> ).
                Given a circular log of 14 inches in diameter, the maximum
                strength for a beam with width <m>w</m> is given by:
                <md>
                    k = \frac{196}{3w^2}
                </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine the sensitivity function <m>S(k,w)</m> that
                    captures the sensitivity of <m>k</m> to changes in the
                    constant <m>w</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the sensitivity when <m>w = 13</m> and
                    <m>k = 0.39</m>?
                    Interpret the meaning of your calculation in the context of
                    the scenario.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What conclusions can you make about how confident we can be
                    in the output of this model?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <p>
        <term> Retake of Quiz 2:</term>
    </p>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                After they were protected from hunting, the elephants in Kruger
                National Park (South Africa) experienced birth rates exceeding
                death rates for 60 years.
                The researchers at Kruger National Park determined that the
                growth rate (birth rate-death rate) is at its maximum in 1983
                where <m>t=0</m> in 1903.
                A model of the elephant population from 1903 to 2013 is given by
                the logistic function
                <md>
                    f(t) = \frac{C}{1+e^{\frac{k-t}{8}}},
                </md>
                where <m>t</m> is years, <m>f(t)</m> is in thousands per year,
                and <m>C</m> and <m>k</m> are positive parameters.
            </p>

            <image source="images/quiz2_2.png" width="40%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Graph of the elephant population in Kruger National Park
                    from 1903 to 2013.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Describe what this model shows is happening to the elephant
                    population in the long term.
                    Identify any connection to the parameter <m>C</m> and what
                    you think its value is.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Identify an inflection point by circling it directly on the
                    graph above.
                    Describe its significance in the context of the model.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <p>
        <term> Retake of Quiz 1:</term>
    </p>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Name the stages of the life-cycle of mathematical
                modeling.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose you are given the task of determining whether you should go
                out of the way to get cheaper gas. Or get the gas that is more
                expensive, but next to your work.
                What modeling question might you ask?
                What assumptions would you need to make?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>